WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:03.270
Film Courage: How do you feel about the quality
of storytelling you’re seeing today?

00:00:03.270 --> 00:00:05.910
Jack Perez, Director: I mean…that’s a
good question.

00:00:05.910 --> 00:00:10.740
How do I feel about the quality?

00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:11.740
I don’t know?

00:00:11.740 --> 00:00:15.960
I’m not really attracted to a lot of new
stuff.

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:22.199
I think because I’ve seen a lot of it before
and that’s just my personal thing.

00:00:22.199 --> 00:00:29.090
I think at this point it’s just an age thing
and it’s a result of being a film nerd because

00:00:29.090 --> 00:00:35.329
when you consume as much cinema as I have
in my lifetime you have seen (in most cases)

00:00:35.329 --> 00:00:40.140
the best iterations or story or genre already.

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:48.329
So when I see something new that just manages
to be good execution or well executed story

00:00:48.329 --> 00:00:51.239
that I’ve already seen it’s not enough
for me already.

00:00:51.239 --> 00:01:00.500
So I find myself going back to the films that
have really changed my life, that really did

00:01:00.500 --> 00:01:02.910
it best because I want to be nourished because
in that way.

00:01:02.910 --> 00:01:07.250
It’s not enough for me to see something
that is just a really good yarn that was well-directed

00:01:07.250 --> 00:01:12.900
and well-produced even though that’s a monumental
feat (any filmmaker will tell you that making

00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:14.909
a good movie is a miracle).

00:01:14.909 --> 00:01:21.299
But for me personally I tend to look to the
past for the inspiration because I feel like

00:01:21.299 --> 00:01:28.420
in most cases it was done best and that doesn’t
mean that I’m resistant to somebody showing

00:01:28.420 --> 00:01:31.670
me something that is truly brilliant because
I would welcome that.

00:01:31.670 --> 00:01:36.540
If somebody showed me something that was earth
shatteringly brilliant better than anything

00:01:36.540 --> 00:01:38.439
else then I would be like be like “Jesus
Christ!

00:01:38.439 --> 00:01:39.439
Thank you!”

00:01:39.439 --> 00:01:42.030
And I want to see that stuff and I’m sure
it’s out there, I’m sure it’s out there.

00:01:42.030 --> 00:01:43.710
I just haven’t seen anything lately.

00:01:43.710 --> 00:01:46.673
Film Courage: Well I know THE HANDMAID’S
TALE, it was a movie with Elizabeth McGovern,

00:01:46.673 --> 00:01:49.840
was it the eighties I think?

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:51.110
Or early nineties was it?

00:01:51.110 --> 00:01:52.110
Jack: Yes?

00:01:52.110 --> 00:01:53.110
The movie?

00:01:53.110 --> 00:01:54.399
You mean the original HANDMAID’S TALE?

00:01:54.399 --> 00:02:01.619
Film Courage: And so now…now…it’s got
a whole new life to it and they are spoofing

00:02:01.619 --> 00:02:04.150
it on Saturday Night Life.

00:02:04.150 --> 00:02:08.390
But back then I don’t know if I had actually
heard a lot about it.

00:02:08.390 --> 00:02:10.259
Jack: I think it was in the eighties.

00:02:10.259 --> 00:02:15.040
I was actually an intern at the production
company where they were making that movie.

00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:17.599
I remember Xeroxing…f*cking up the Xerox.

00:02:17.599 --> 00:02:24.620
I forget who was the director of that, a German
director?

00:02:24.620 --> 00:02:31.400
But anyway it was in New York City, I was
like a PA in the office and they were having

00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:36.650
this special lunch meeting or something and
I was in charge of getting the new draft together

00:02:36.650 --> 00:02:41.060
and I was like printing this thing out and
it was just jammed in the printer and I ran

00:02:41.060 --> 00:02:44.980
over to 21 or wherever they were having dinner
and I remember giving it to the producer and

00:02:44.980 --> 00:02:48.200
it was like all the pages were out of order
and he was looking at me judgmentally and

00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:52.069
I was like “I’m f*cked, I’m never going
to work in this business again.”

00:02:52.069 --> 00:03:01.870
Well like a good story that a filmmaker or
a writer finds a way in or changes it or makes

00:03:01.870 --> 00:03:11.780
it more contemporary or more resonate or personal
(whatever it is) I think that’s totally

00:03:11.780 --> 00:03:14.160
valid, too.

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:16.420
But I haven’t seen much recently.

00:03:16.420 --> 00:03:25.010
Film Courage: It’s kind of like a band that
does like…When the Levee Breaks and it’s

00:03:25.010 --> 00:03:27.940
like okay…it sounded so much better when
Robert Plant sang it.

00:03:27.940 --> 00:03:34.120
But now you’re doing it but it just doesn’t
have the same appeal…but again is it a generational

00:03:34.120 --> 00:03:41.220
thing because you and I were just talking
off camera about video discs and seeing even

00:03:41.220 --> 00:03:44.519
nineties films that had a different feel to
it.

00:03:44.519 --> 00:03:46.280
Jack: Well I think that’s true.

00:03:46.280 --> 00:03:48.030
I talk to my students about this all the time.

00:03:48.030 --> 00:03:50.920
I think it’s an amazing age and it’s a
horrible age.

00:03:50.920 --> 00:03:54.060
It’s amazing in the sense that anything
that you want to see in the history of the

00:03:54.060 --> 00:03:57.900
world is available like that.

00:03:57.900 --> 00:04:02.650
Gone are the days of hunting for a revival
house that is going to be showing THE THIRD

00:04:02.650 --> 00:04:07.299
MAN or SEVENTH SAMURAI or what have you or
anything for that matter.

00:04:07.299 --> 00:04:12.909
It’s not that you have to hunt for a video
store (not that they exist anymore) that is

00:04:12.909 --> 00:04:17.670
going to carry a special John Woo Hong King
action thing, one that you can’t find.

00:04:17.670 --> 00:04:20.050
Everything is available at any given moment.

00:04:20.050 --> 00:04:25.280
That said there is so much material, there
is so much available that I wouldn’t know

00:04:25.280 --> 00:04:26.970
where to begin.

00:04:26.970 --> 00:04:32.730
And I think that that’s a problem because
unless there is somebody (a teacher or film

00:04:32.730 --> 00:04:38.150
geek parent) that is saying “Spend your
time watching this…this…this and this

00:04:38.150 --> 00:04:42.530
before you watch this other stuff because
that’s going to educate you, that’s going

00:04:42.530 --> 00:04:45.170
to make you a better filmmaker.”

00:04:45.170 --> 00:04:49.970
Then you’re bound to lose those great things
and I’m not even talking about the obvious

00:04:49.970 --> 00:04:56.410
classics, I’m just talking about the minor
films that are still better than the junk

00:04:56.410 --> 00:05:01.380
you see today, you’re never going to see
them because there’s just too much to look

00:05:01.380 --> 00:05:03.050
at.

00:05:03.050 --> 00:05:11.920
I’m sort of losing the thread of the question
but my feeling now is there is almost too

00:05:11.920 --> 00:05:21.450
much to see and it’s almost as if you need
to graduate, that there is a prerequisite.

00:05:21.450 --> 00:05:24.170
You have to watch all this stuff if you want
to be a filmmaker and then you can watch as

00:05:24.170 --> 00:05:26.650
much junk as you want that is out now.

00:05:26.650 --> 00:05:34.360
Film Courage: Right well a lot of stuff seems
over processed, too over-produced in some

00:05:34.360 --> 00:05:35.360
ways?

00:05:35.360 --> 00:05:38.650
And maybe just as a culture we’ve become
use to that so if it’s not packaged that

00:05:38.650 --> 00:05:40.250
way we don’t want it?

00:05:40.250 --> 00:05:43.490
Jack: I think you’re right, we were talking
about this earlier.

00:05:43.490 --> 00:05:50.340
If social media is overproduced (if someone’s
personal Instagram page with all it requisite

00:05:50.340 --> 00:06:00.000
filters and glamour and the tools of production
in the hands of anybody, it raises this level,

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:06.450
this standard of what’s acceptable to a
professional level in such a way.

00:06:06.450 --> 00:06:08.370
But it also creates a certain artifice.

00:06:08.370 --> 00:06:15.820
One of my favorite quotes that John Cassavetes
said about Hollywood films in general is that

00:06:15.820 --> 00:06:18.510
“They are an advertisement for life.”

00:06:18.510 --> 00:06:25.080
That what he said “Hollywood movies in most
cases are an advertisement for life.

00:06:25.080 --> 00:06:33.630
Whereas my films which are clearly, comparatively
very rough and mistakenly looked upon as almost

00:06:33.630 --> 00:06:41.180
amateurish compared to that polish and mistakenly
because that was by design because he was

00:06:41.180 --> 00:06:42.180
a professional.

00:06:42.180 --> 00:06:44.390
He made these films look a certain way.

00:06:44.390 --> 00:06:46.740
Because he was trying to get to something
that was the opposite, he was trying to get

00:06:46.740 --> 00:06:51.710
at the messiness, the reality of life.

00:06:51.710 --> 00:06:59.940
Whereas Hollywood almost by definition tries
to minimize that in favor of this gloss or

00:06:59.940 --> 00:07:04.740
polish or illusion.

00:07:04.740 --> 00:07:10.620
And that becomes the diet that you’re consuming.

00:07:10.620 --> 00:07:17.070
If you see something that is gritty or messy
or whatever and I’m not even talking about

00:07:17.070 --> 00:07:18.070
the look.

00:07:18.070 --> 00:07:22.250
I’m talking about the content, where the
content is not just this clearly, safe, defined

00:07:22.250 --> 00:07:23.400
thing.

00:07:23.400 --> 00:07:26.520
People may have a resistance to that where
they may go “This is too much.

00:07:26.520 --> 00:07:27.840
I don’t want this.

00:07:27.840 --> 00:07:32.040
This is not the food I’ve chosen to eat.”

00:07:32.040 --> 00:07:34.090
But what does it do to you tastes?

00:07:34.090 --> 00:07:42.710
It limits your taste to only want this candy
and not something that is more substantial

00:07:42.710 --> 00:07:43.710
in that way…nourishing.

00:07:43.710 --> 00:07:47.990
Film Courage: I was just watching part of
THE STEPFORD WIVES.

00:07:47.990 --> 00:07:49.390
Jack: Which one?

00:07:49.390 --> 00:07:50.390
The original.

00:07:50.390 --> 00:07:51.910
Film Courage: Yes.

00:07:51.910 --> 00:07:59.190
And just like the long driving sequence beginning
with all the credits and the classic early

00:07:59.190 --> 00:08:06.120
seventies music that we would hear all the
time and now it sounds so wonderful but every

00:08:06.120 --> 00:08:08.270
show and movie had it.

00:08:08.270 --> 00:08:12.050
But just in terms of how quiet it is.

00:08:12.050 --> 00:08:16.890
There are just moments where nothing is happening
every second, it’s breathing.

00:08:16.890 --> 00:08:21.660
Jack: Yes and I’ve always said this and
anybody will tell you this that the strength

00:08:21.660 --> 00:08:27.330
of a spike in story or movie where somebody
goes crazy, there’s a lot of kinetic activity

00:08:27.330 --> 00:08:34.190
or surprise or whatever is directly related
to the quiet that precedes it.

00:08:34.190 --> 00:08:39.340
In other words something is super loud or
big relative to what preceded it.

00:08:39.340 --> 00:08:44.390
So that’s why in a horror movie (they learned
this going back to the 1930’s) but if you

00:08:44.390 --> 00:08:49.780
have a very quiet almost nothing happening
and then a huge scare, that scare becomes

00:08:49.780 --> 00:08:50.780
magnified.

00:08:50.780 --> 00:08:58.060
Whereas if you have to maintain what is now
the classic, Hollywood mantra of just a thrill

00:08:58.060 --> 00:09:06.030
every second, then that thrill every second
becomes a plateau, you just become accustomed

00:09:06.030 --> 00:09:07.370
to it.

00:09:07.370 --> 00:09:14.240
It’s not going to hit you the same way then
if you allowed for that quiet to proceed it.

00:09:14.240 --> 00:09:19.010
But that assumes that the audience…well
it probably assumes that the producers are

00:09:19.010 --> 00:09:22.000
will to allow that quiet to happen and not
be “Oh sh*t!

00:09:22.000 --> 00:09:24.430
People are going to be bored, we have to do
something exciting.

00:09:24.430 --> 00:09:28.080
We can’t have this length of quiet.”

00:09:28.080 --> 00:09:33.061
And that the audiences are willing to invest
in that, be okay with that.

00:09:33.061 --> 00:09:34.080
And to say “This is not boring.

00:09:34.080 --> 00:09:35.100
This is interesting.”

00:09:35.100 --> 00:09:40.000
And then be the beneficiary of the result
of that if something huge follows that because

00:09:40.000 --> 00:09:42.870
it will be huger because of it.

